          "prompt": "Explain how src/inventory/reorder.py decides what to order.",
          "answer": "Based on reading `src/inventory/reorder.py`, here's how the reorder policy works:\n\n## Two functions determine what and how much to order:\n\n### `reorder_due()` \u2014 Which items need ordering\n- Returns **every SKU** whose current stock level is at or below `REORDER_THRESHOLD` (12, from `src/core/config.py`)\n- Iterates over all items via `all_items()`, checks each with `level_for(item[\"sku\"])`, and includes it if `level <= 12`\n\n### `order_size(sku)` \u2014 How many to order for a given SKU\n- **Formula**: `shortfall = REORDER_THRESHOLD * 3 - level_for(sku)`\n  - Aims to bring stock up to **3\u00d7 the reorder threshold** (i.e., 36 units)\n  - Subtracts the current level to compute the shortfall\n- **Caps the order at `MAX_BATCH`** (250, also from config):\n  ```python\n  return min(max(shortfall, 0), MAX_BATCH)\n  ```\n  - `max(shortfall, 0)` prevents negative orders (if stock is already above threshold)\n  - `min(..., MAX_BATCH)` caps at 250 units maximum\n\n## In summary:\n- **Trigger**: Items at or below 12 units\n- **Target**: Restock to 36 units (3 \u00d7 threshold)  \n- **Cap**: Never order more than 250 units at once",
